# Migrating `TimedExecutor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. The entire cancellation model must change.

## .NET 8 Replacement

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block until completion or timeout
            if (task.Wait(timeout))
                return task.Result;

            cts.Cancel();
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerException is not null)
        {
            // Unwrap single inner exceptions to preserve original behavior
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerExceptions);
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async version with cooperative cancellation
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Issue | Detail |
|-------|--------|
| **No forced termination** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, there's no way to forcibly kill work in .NET 8. If `work` ignores cancellation, it continues running after timeout. |
| **`Task.Run` + `CancellationToken`** | The token only prevents *scheduling*—it doesn't interrupt already-running code. |
| **Resource leaks** | The old code killed the thread; now the work may complete in the background. Dispose patterns must account for this. |
| **`default(T)` race** | The original had a subtle race on `result`—`Task.Run` eliminates this. |
| **`ThreadAbortException` removal** | Any downstream `catch (ThreadAbortException)` blocks become dead code—remove them. |

## If You Must Hard-Kill Uncooperative Code

For legacy code that can't be made cancellation-aware:

```csharp
// Last resort: run in a separate process
public T ExecuteWithHardTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    var task = Task.Run(work);

    if (task.Wait(timeout))
        return task.Result;

    // The work is now orphaned—log a warning
    _ = task.ContinueWith(t =>
    {
        if (t.IsFaulted)
            Console.Error.WriteLine($"Orphaned task faulted: {t.Exception}");
    }, TaskContinuationOptions.OnlyOnFaulted);

    throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
}
```

## Migration Checklist

1. Remove all `Thread.Abort()` / `ResetAbort()` calls
2. Replace `ThreadAbortException` catches with `OperationCanceledException`
3. Pass `CancellationToken` through your call chain (preferred)
4. Audit callers—they may depend on the guarantee that work *stops* on timeout (no longer true)
5. For I/O-bound work, use async APIs that accept `CancellationToken` natively
